{"results":[{"entries":[{"dbId":"373856","stId":"R-HSA-373856","id":"R-HSA-373856","name":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCL9</span>","type":"Protein","exactType":"ReferenceGeneProduct","species":["Homo sapiens"],"referenceName":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCL9</span>","referenceIdentifier":"Q07325","compartmentNames":["extracellular region"],"compartmentAccession":["0005576"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":true,"databaseName":"UniProt","referenceURL":"http://purl.uniprot.org/uniprot/Q07325","disease":false},{"dbId":"9943832","stId":"R-HSA-9943832","id":"R-HSA-9943832","name":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span>","type":"Protein","exactType":"ReferenceIsoform","species":["Homo sapiens"],"referenceName":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span>","referenceIdentifier":"P49682","compartmentNames":["plasma membrane"],"compartmentAccession":["0005886"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":true,"databaseName":"UniProt","referenceURL":"http://purl.uniprot.org/uniprot/P49682-1","disease":false},{"dbId":"2980699","stId":"R-HSA-2980699","id":"R-HSA-2980699","name":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span>","type":"Protein","exactType":"ReferenceGeneProduct","species":["Homo sapiens"],"referenceName":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span>","referenceIdentifier":"P49682","compartmentNames":["plasma membrane"],"compartmentAccession":["0005886"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":true,"databaseName":"UniProt","referenceURL":"http://purl.uniprot.org/uniprot/P49682","disease":false}],"typeName":"Protein","entriesCount":3,"rowCount":3},{"entries":[{"dbId":"374248","stId":"R-HSA-374248","id":"R-HSA-374248","name":"Receptor <span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span> binds ligands <span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCL9</span> to 11","type":"Reaction","exactType":"binding","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span> (Loetscher M et al, 1996) is predominantly expressed on T lymphocytes, and on other lymphocytes (some B cells and NK cells). It is highly induced following cell activation. There are three isoforms, <span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span>-A, <span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span>-B and <span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span>-alt. <span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span> binds to three highly related ligands in mammals, <span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCL9</span>, CXCL10 and CXCL11 (Xanthou G et al, 2003). The ligands then elicit their chemoattractant functions. <span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCL9</span> (CMK; monokine induced by gamma-interferon, MIG; SCYB9) is a T cell chemoattractant (Farber JM, 1993). CXCL10 (IP-10) (Booth V et al, 2002) is secreted by monocytes, endothelial cells and fibroblasts in response to gamma-interferon. It can act as a chemoattractant for monocytes, macrophages, T cells, NK cells and dendritic cells, promote T cell adhesion to endothelial cells and inhibit angiogenesis and bone marrow colony formation. CXCL11 (I-TAC,; iIP-9) (Cole KE et al, 1998) is highly expressed in peripheral blood leukocytes, pancreas and liver. It is regulated by interferon and has potent chemoattractant activity for interleukin-2-activated T cells.","compartmentNames":["extracellular region","plasma membrane"],"compartmentAccession":["0005576","0005886"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9942930","stId":"R-HSA-9942930","id":"R-HSA-9942930","name":"TBX21 binds the C-X-C chemokine receptor type 3 (<span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span>) gene","type":"Reaction","exactType":"binding","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"TBX21 binds the promoter of the C‑X‑C chemokine receptor type 3 (<span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span>) gene (Kanhere et al. 2012, inferred from human B cells in Beima et al. 2006, and inferred from mouse homologs). In mice, Tbx21 binds the 5′ enhancer of <span class=\"highlighting\" >Cxcr3</span>, about 3 kb upstream of the <span class=\"highlighting\" >Cxcr3</span> transcription start site (Zhu et al. 2012). Without T-bet, H3K4me1 modification is reduced around this binding site (Zhu et al. 2012).","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9942931","stId":"R-HSA-9942931","id":"R-HSA-9942931","name":"TBX21 positively regulates expression of C-X-C chemokine receptor type 3 (<span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span>)","type":"Reaction","exactType":"omitted","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"TBX21 directly upregulates expression of the chemokine receptor <span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span> in T helper 1 cells (Th1 cells) (inferred from mouse homologs in Szabo et al. 2000, Lord et al. 2005, Zhu et al. 2012). T-bet binds to the 5′ enhancer of the <span class=\"highlighting\" >Cxcr3</span>, and without T-bet, H3K4me1 modification is reduced around this binding site (Zhu et al. 2012). Human Th1 cells preferentially express <span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span> and CCR5 chemokine receptors (Bonecchi et al. 1998). <span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span> binds three chemokines that are induced by type I interferons: <span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCL9</span> (MIG), CXCL10 (IP‑10), and CXCL11 (I‑TAC) (reviewed in Chu et al. 2023, Rubinstein et al. 2024) to induce the migration of Th1 cells into inflammatory sites (reviewed in Groom and Luster 2011, Zhang et al. 2014).","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm","plasma membrane"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654","0005886"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9942928","stId":"R-HSA-9942928","id":"R-HSA-9942928","name":"TBX21 positively regulates expression of Protein-tyrosine sulfotransferase 2 (TPST2)","type":"Reaction","exactType":"omitted","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"In T helper 1 cells (Th1 cells), TBX21 upregulates expression of Protein‑tyrosine sulfotransferase 2 (TPST2) (inferred from mouse homologs in Lord et al. 2005). TPST1 and/or TPST2 catalyze the sulfation of tyrosine residues in the N‑terminus of the cytokine receptor <span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span>, increasing its binding activity to some ligands (Gao et al. 2009). TPST2 also causes PSGL1 post-translational modifications required for binding activity, which is linked to the ability of Th1 cells to migrate and access inflamed skin (Pritchard et al 2019).","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false}],"typeName":"Reaction","entriesCount":4,"rowCount":4},{"entries":[{"dbId":"9942921","stId":"R-HSA-9942921","id":"R-HSA-9942921","name":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span> gene","type":"DNA Sequence","exactType":"ReferenceDNASequence","species":["Homo sapiens"],"referenceIdentifier":"ENSEMBL:ENSG00000186810","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":true,"databaseName":"ENSEMBL","referenceURL":"https://www.ensembl.org/id/ENSG00000186810","disease":false}],"typeName":"DNA Sequence","entriesCount":1,"rowCount":1},{"entries":[{"dbId":"374233","stId":"R-HSA-374233","id":"R-HSA-374233","name":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span> ligands","type":"Set","exactType":"DefinedSet","species":["Homo sapiens"],"compartmentNames":["extracellular region"],"compartmentAccession":["0005576"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false}],"typeName":"Set","entriesCount":1,"rowCount":1},{"entries":[{"dbId":"374141","stId":"R-HSA-374141","id":"R-HSA-374141","name":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span>:<span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span> ligands","type":"Complex","exactType":"Complex","species":["Homo sapiens"],"compartmentNames":["plasma membrane"],"compartmentAccession":["0005886"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9942937","stId":"R-HSA-9942937","id":"R-HSA-9942937","name":"TBX21:<span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span> gene","type":"Complex","exactType":"Complex","species":["Homo sapiens"],"compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false}],"typeName":"Complex","entriesCount":2,"rowCount":2},{"entries":[{"dbId":"9942503","stId":"R-HSA-9942503","id":"R-HSA-9942503","name":"Differentiation of naive CD4+ T cells to T helper 1 cells (Th1 cells)","type":"Pathway","exactType":"Pathway","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"A conjunction of signaling from the T cell receptor, Interferon gamma (IFNG), and Interleukin-12 (IL12) induces the differentiation of T helper 1 cells (Th1) by activating the master regulator TBX21 (T-bet). Expression of TBX21 (T-bet) occurs in two phases. The first phase is dependent on IFNG acting via STAT1 and the T cell receptor acting via calcium, calcineurin, and NFATC1 (inferred from mouse homologs in Zhong et al. 2025). During the first phase, TBX21 activates expression of IL21RB2, a subunit of the receptor for IL12 (inferred from mouse homologs in Mullen et al. 2001, Afkarian et al. 2002, Schulz et al. 2009, Zhu et al. 2012). The second phase is dependent on IL12 acting via IL21RB2 and STAT4 (Inferred from mouse cells in Schulz et al. 2009, Wei et al. 2010, Zhu et al. 2012, Fang et al. 2022).<br>TBX21 with RUNX3 then enhances expression of IFNG (inferred from mouse homologs in Szabo et al. 2000, Mullen et al. 2001, Hatton et al. 2006, Djuretic et al. 2007, Jenner et al. 2009, Zhu et al. 2012), thereby creating a positive feedback loop that maintains TBX21 expression. TBX21 also binds the transcription factor RUNX1 and prevents RUNX1 from activating expression of RORC, an activator of Th17 differentiation (inferred from mouse homologs in Lazarevic et al. 2011). TBX21 also represses expression of IL4, an activator of Th2 differentiation (Jenner et al. 2009, and inferred from mouse homologs in Zhu et al. 2012). Thus, TBX21 restricts naive CD4+ T cells to the Th1 fate.<br>TBX21 then directly activates expression of Th1 regulators and cell markers, including C-X-C chemokine receptor type 3 (<span class=\"highlighting\" >CXCR3</span>) (inferred from mouse homologs in Szabo et al. 2000, Lord et al. 2005, Zhu et al. 2012, Gökmen et al. 2013), Tumor necrosis factor (TNF, TNFA) (Jenner et al. 2009, and inferred from mouse homologs in Zhu et al. 2012), C-C motif chemokine 3 (CCL3) (Jenner et al. 2009, and inferred from mouse homologs in supplement-02 of Zhu et al. 2012). TBX21 also upregulates expression of Protein‑tyrosine sulfotransferase 2 (TPST2) (inferred from mouse homologs in Lord et al. 2005), which is linked to the ability of Th1 cells to migrate and access inflamed skin (Pritchard et al. 2019).","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"hasEHLD":false,"disease":false}],"typeName":"Pathway","entriesCount":1,"rowCount":1}],"rowCount":12,"numberOfGroups":6,"numberOfMatches":12}